+\section*{About this edition}
+\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{About this edition}
+
+\noindent\textbf{First edition.} This volume brings the realization model, institutional theory, and accountability analysis into one reference treatment. Its version number identifies an editorial and source release. It does not turn an open research question into a theorem or substitute for independent mathematical review.
+
+\subsection*{The argument in five parts}
+
+\noindent\textbf{I. Realization and abstraction.} What state is present, what an observer can access, and when a smaller description preserves dynamics and interventions. These sections supply the mathematical language used by the remainder of the volume.
+
+\noindent\textbf{II. Institutions and collective choice.} How authority, membership, communicative acts, incentives, constitutional revision, and distributed information constrain the operations of a plural population.
+
+\noindent\textbf{III. Continuity, compatibility, and knowledge.} What persists through replacement, how local rules and meanings compose, and why cultural accumulation, coherent testimony, and common knowledge require different conditions.
+
+\noindent\textbf{IV. Authority and joint feasibility.} Which participants actually control consequential transitions, how staffing and shared resources constrain a common policy, and which finite witnesses certify continuation and service.
+
+\noindent\textbf{V. Accountability and the limits of persistence.} How external claims, strategic inspection, confidentiality, appeals, authorized endings, and changing certificate assumptions enter the same operational contract. The concluding sections distinguish settled finite results from further questions.
+
+\subsection*{Reading routes}
+
+A conceptual reading can begin with Sections~\ref{sec:introduction}--\ref{sec:commons}, continue through Sections~\ref{sec:society}--\ref{sec:language}, and then read the interpretations in Sections~\ref{sec:joint-synthesis} and~\ref{sec:accountability}. A mathematical reading follows the parts in order. An implementation review can begin with the exact contracts in Sections~\ref{sec:governance}, \ref{sec:joint-enforcement}, \ref{sec:joint-synthesis}, and~\ref{sec:accountability}, then consult the theorem index and bounded reference programs in the source package.
+
+\subsection*{Status of the claims}
+
+Definitions specify a contract. Propositions and theorems carry conditional prose proofs. Some are explicit applications of classical results. Counterexamples separate claims that would otherwise be easy to conflate. Design proposals and open questions are not promoted to conclusions by the notation used to express them. The companion programs check finite constructions, not arbitrary deployed populations. No formal proof-assistant verification or independent peer review is claimed for this edition.
+
+The work analyzes possible computational institutions. It does not infer from their feasibility that they should be deployed, acquire authority over people, or be treated as having established another party's consent. The choice of protected claims and the justification of authority remain explicit inputs to the analysis.
