+\subsection{Accountable continuation}
+A common policy is still incomplete when its boundary omits an affected party, its audit is not performed, or its certificate is used after the supporting assumptions change. Part V places these issues inside the realization model. It derives a sharp privacy envelope for bounded enforcement, gives an exact finite criterion for maintaining an available authorized ending, and states a guarded rule for changing certificate modes. The same resource accounting also applies to effective appeals. This makes accountability part of the theory's common-policy question rather than an assurance stated outside it.
 
 \section{Realization, formation, and advantage}\label{sec:scope}
 Three questions organize the analysis. \emph{Realizability} asks whether a specified organization can be implemented. \emph{Formation} asks whether interactions select, establish, or revise its structure. \emph{Advantage} asks whether it improves a declared outcome against a constrained comparison class. Constructing a system answers none of the latter questions automatically. An executable rule can be externally imposed, locally adopted, or maintained by strategic agreement. These are different histories of the same possible implementation.
