+\clearpage\part{Accountability and the limits of persistence}
+\section{Accountability, revision, and authorized endings}\label{sec:accountability}
+
+An institution can continue to operate while becoming less answerable to the parties it affects. It can preserve a rule whose authority has expired, fund sanctions but not appeals, or treat unfinished obligations as a reason never to accept an authorized ending. None of these possibilities is excluded by persistence alone. The remaining question is therefore not simply whether a computational organization survives. It is whether the same realized organization respects the limits of its authority, supports effective correction, and can enter a specified suspension or settlement state.
+
+This section extends the common-policy question of Section~\ref{sec:joint-synthesis}. External claims, auditor incentives, confidentiality, review capacity, stopping procedures, and the continuing applicability of certificates must constrain one implementation. They are not additional benefits that can be asserted after the implementation has consumed its entire budget. Nor are they consequences of selecting the word \emph{society}. Each is an explicit part of a contract whose normative selection remains separate from its mathematical feasibility.
+
+\subsection{Affected outsiders and the boundary of authority}\label{sec:external-effects}
+
+Membership, decision rights, resource control, and exposure to consequences define different sets. Let $M$ denote participating members, $V$ positions with specified decision rights, $H$ infrastructure controllers, and $E$ affected parties outside the membership. These sets may overlap where the description allows it. A human resource owner can be in $H$ without holding an internal vote. A person whose reserved service is delayed can be in $E$ without ever communicating with the agents. Conversely, membership does not confer authority over every affected resource.
+
+A useful external-effects contract records
+\[
+\mathcal B=(Z_{\rm ext},O_{\rm ext},\mathsf{Auth},\mathsf{Effect},
+\mathsf{Standing},\mathsf{Remedy}).
+\]
+Here $Z_{\rm ext}$ contains the external facts relevant to the guarantee, $O_{\rm ext}$ specifies which of them can be observed, $\mathsf{Auth}$ identifies the stipulated authorization for each boundary-crossing act, and $\mathsf{Effect}$ includes the consequences covered by the model. $\mathsf{Standing}$ specifies who may initiate a challenge and on which grounds. $\mathsf{Remedy}$ specifies the actions an authorized review can actually require. The contract can use a finite abstraction of external state, but it must justify that abstraction relative to the protected questions.
+
+This is a representation proposal, not a universal allocation of voting rights. Recognizing an affected party does not automatically give that party an unlimited veto. Conversely, omitting a party from $M$ does not establish consent. The source of authority must select the applicable protections openly. Research on meaningful human control distinguishes responsiveness to relevant human reasons from traceability to responsible human actors~\cite{meaningfulcontrol}. The operational question here is how such requirements enter the action, evidence, and resource contract of the same computation.
+
+\begin{counterexample}[Exact internal accounting with an omitted external claim]\label{ex:external-claim}
+Four execution slots exist. The internal state contains four member requests and a rule granting one slot to each. In realization $h_0$, all four slots are available to that institution. In realization $h_1$, an external owner has reserved one slot for a nonmember, and has authorized the institution to use only the other three. Suppose the reservation is absent from every internal record. The same four grants satisfy the internal predicate $\sum_iq_i\le4$ in both realizations, but exceed the institution's stipulated authority in $h_1$. Perfect preservation of the internal state cannot distinguish the cases.
+\end{counterexample}
+
+The failure is not an arithmetic error. It is an omitted premise about what the institution is entitled to allocate. Proposition~\ref{prop:founding} supplies the corresponding information criterion: an internal record can determine the reservation fact only if that fact is constant on its record fibres. A signed statement can improve the evidence only under a stated trust model. It does not manufacture the owner's authorization.
+
+The example also distinguishes \emph{impact} from \emph{authority}. An institution can affect an outsider while lacking authority to do so, and a permitted action can have effects beyond those its designers anticipated. An exact certificate relative to a boundary cannot establish that the boundary includes all consequential effects. Unknown external dependencies remain a modeling risk. Extending the state description is necessary when an omitted claim matters, but simply adding a variable without an observation or authorization mechanism is not a repair.
+
+\subsection{Inspection as a strategic service}\label{sec:strategic-auditor}
+
+The earlier enforcement calculations assume that an announced observation and sanction policy is carried out. A strategic auditor makes that assumption visible. Inspection games and the distinction between simultaneous inspection and committed inspector behavior have an established game-theoretic treatment~\cite{stengelinspection}. The following one-period game is a deliberately small instance, not the recursive model of that source.
+
+A participant chooses compliance $C$ or deviation $D$. An auditor simultaneously chooses no inspection $N$ or inspection $I$. Deviation gives the participant gross gain $g>0$. An inspection detects this specified deviation perfectly and imposes an authorized, collectible penalty $F>g$. The auditor pays cost $c>0$ to inspect and receives reward $b>c$ only on detection. There are no false accusations, side payments, further continuation payoffs, or prior commitment to inspect. Payoffs are risk-neutral and common knowledge. The ordered pair in each entry is the participant's payoff followed by the auditor's payoff.
+
+\begin{center}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
+\toprule
+ & No inspection $N$ & Inspection $I$\\
+\midrule
+Compliance $C$ & $(0,0)$ & $(0,-c)$\\
+Deviation $D$ & $(g,0)$ & $(g-F,b-c)$\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+
+\begin{proposition}[Inspection without commitment]\label{prop:strategic-inspector}
+Under the preceding strict inequalities, the game has no pure Nash equilibrium and has a unique Nash equilibrium in independently mixed strategies. Its deviation and inspection probabilities are
+\[
+p^*=\frac{c}{b},\qquad q^*=\frac{g}{F}.
+\]
+In particular, certain compliance is not sustained in this simultaneous game.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+If the auditor inspects with probability $q$, the participant's advantage from deviation is $g-qF$. If the participant deviates with probability $p$, the auditor's advantage from inspection is $pb-c$. At $(C,N)$ the participant prefers $D$, at $(D,N)$ the auditor prefers $I$, at $(D,I)$ the participant prefers $C$, and at $(C,I)$ the auditor prefers $N$. Thus no pure profile is an equilibrium. If either player uses a pure strategy, the other's strict best response yields one of these nonequilibrium pure profiles. Both players must therefore mix. Their two indifference equations uniquely give the displayed probabilities, which lie strictly between zero and one and make every action in each support a best response.
+\end{proof}
+
+The residual violation probability does not come from an inaccurate detector. When everyone complies, an auditor paid only for detections prefers not to incur the cost. When nobody inspects, deviation is profitable. It would be incorrect to insert the inspector's optimal response to certain deviation into a certificate that simultaneously assumes certain compliance. These responses belong to different strategic situations.
+
+A different payment contract changes the result. Suppose every \emph{genuinely performed and independently verifiable} inspection receives fee $w>c$, with the detection reward otherwise unchanged. Inspection then has advantage $w+pb-c>0$ at every $p$. The auditor strictly prefers $I$, and the participant strictly prefers $C$ because $F>g$. The unique equilibrium is $(C,I)$. At $w=c$, inspection can be a weak best response to compliance, but the strict implementation claim is lost. This extension requires an affordable fee and a mechanism that verifies the performed service. Paying for an unverified assertion that an inspection occurred does not satisfy the assumption.
+
+The two calculations are conditional alternatives. They do not solve collusion, fabrication by the receipt checker, repeated bargaining, unavailable penalties, or disagreement about legitimate enforcement. A commitment mechanism may move some of these requirements to a different institution. The new institution and its resource costs must then enter the common contract rather than disappear from it.
+
+\subsection{Confidentiality limits the distinctions available to enforcement}\label{sec:privacy-enforcement}
+
+An institution may have good reasons not to collect or disclose every private state. The relevant question is which distinctions a protected record may reveal, not whether more information is always desirable. Let $P_C$ and $P_D$ be the laws of the \emph{complete record available to a sanctioning procedure}. For $\lambda\ge1$, impose
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:pairwise-privacy}
+ P_D(E)\le\lambda P_C(E),\qquad P_C(E)\le\lambda P_D(E)
+ \quad\text{for every event }E.
+\end{equation}
+The finite setting makes these conditions equivalent to the corresponding pointwise inequalities. When the two underlying inputs are neighbors in a declared pure differential-privacy contract, they hold with $\lambda=e^\varepsilon$. The neighboring-input relation is essential. Differential privacy does not require arbitrary acts of compliance and misconduct to be treated as neighboring inputs. Its standard definition and composition principles are described by Dwork and Roth~\cite{dworkroth}.
+
+Keep the earlier definition
+\[
+D_\alpha(P_D,P_C)=\max_{0\le s\le1,\ \E_{P_C}s\le\alpha}
+       (\E_{P_D}s-\E_{P_C}s),\qquad 0\le\alpha\le1.
+\]
+The same randomized sanction $s$ must obey the protected false-positive limit and supply the deviation margin.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Sharp pairwise privacy envelope for a bounded sanction]\label{thm:privacy-envelope}
+For every finite pair satisfying~\eqref{eq:pairwise-privacy},
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:privacy-envelope}
+D_\alpha(P_D,P_C)\le
+ B(\lambda,\alpha):=
+ \min\left\{(\lambda-1)\alpha,\frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda+1}\right\}.
+\end{equation}
+For each $\lambda\ge1$ and $\alpha\in[0,1]$, equality is attained by a pair on two outcomes and a suitable sanction. Consequently, deterrence in the model of Proposition~\ref{prop:deterrence-tv} requires $g\le F B(\lambda,\alpha)$. The envelope is a necessary bound for a specified pair, not a sufficiency claim for every pair satisfying privacy.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Put $a=\E_{P_C}s$ and $b=\E_{P_D}s$. Summing the pointwise inequalities against $s$ gives $b\le\lambda a$. Summing against $1-s$ gives $1-a\le\lambda(1-b)$. Thus
+\[
+b-a\le\min\left\{(\lambda-1)a,
+                   \frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda}(1-a)\right\}.
+\]
+For $\lambda>1$, the first expression increases in $a$ and the second decreases. They are equal at $a=1/(\lambda+1)$. Maximizing their minimum over $0\le a\le\alpha$ gives~\eqref{eq:privacy-envelope}. For $\lambda=1$, the laws coincide and the bound is zero.
+
+For attainment, take outcomes $0,1$ with
+\[
+P_C=\left(\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+1},\frac1{\lambda+1}\right),
+\qquad
+P_D=\left(\frac1{\lambda+1},\frac{\lambda}{\lambda+1}\right).
+\]
+Both privacy inequalities hold. Choose $s(0)=0$ and
+$s(1)=\min\{(\lambda+1)\alpha,1\}$. Its false-positive rate is at most $\alpha$ and its expectation difference is exactly $B(\lambda,\alpha)$. Finally, the incentive inequality is $g\le F(\E_{P_D}s-\E_{P_C}s)$, which yields the stated necessary condition.
+\end{proof}
+
+This is an elementary testing consequence of the stipulated likelihood-ratio constraint, integrated with the existing enforcement model. It does not prescribe weakening confidentiality. Protecting an unrelated private attribute may be compatible with revealing whether a particular authorized operation occurred. An institution can instead change its feasible actions, use a different justified record, reduce the covered gain, or decline the proposed sanction contract. The authority to make those changes is a separate question.
+
+At $\lambda=3$, $\alpha=1/10$, the sharp envelope is $1/5$. With penalty $F=2$, gains above $2/5$ cannot be deterred by any record and sanction obeying these particular simultaneous constraints. The pair $P_C=(3/4,1/4)$ and $P_D=(1/4,3/4)$ with $s=(0,2/5)$ attains that envelope. For a gain $g=1/3$, the incentive margin is $2/5-1/3=1/15$. These are exact rational examples, not inferred observation laws.
+
+\subsection{The full disclosure history is the protected object}\label{sec:disclosure-history}
+
+A private initial report followed by an unrestricted appeal disclosure need not remain private as a whole. Nor does protection of every separate marginal establish protection of their joint transcript. The privacy contract must identify the evaluator and include every record that evaluator can combine, including outputs of subsequent authorized procedures.
+
+\begin{counterexample}[Two uninformative records with an informative joint history]\label{ex:privacy-marginals}
+Let $\theta\in\{0,1\}$ distinguish two protected cases, and let $R$ be a fair independent bit. Release $O_1=R$ and $O_2=R\oplus\theta$. Each marginal is a fair bit under both cases and satisfies~\eqref{eq:pairwise-privacy} with $\lambda=1$. But $O_1\oplus O_2=\theta$. The joint laws have disjoint support and satisfy no finite multiplicative bound.
+\end{counterexample}
+
+There is a useful sufficient accounting rule when each stage is protected \emph{conditionally} on the previously disclosed history. It is the elementary sequential-composition argument, stated here for the exact pair rather than a complete database mechanism.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Conditional disclosure accounting]\label{prop:privacy-transcript}
+Consider $m$ releases on finite alphabets. At each common prefix $h$, suppose their conditional next-release laws $P_C^t(\cdot\mid h)$ and $P_D^t(\cdot\mid h)$ satisfy both pointwise inequalities with factor $\lambda_t\ge1$, uniformly over prefixes that can occur. Then the joint transcript laws satisfy~\eqref{eq:pairwise-privacy} with
+\[
+\lambda=\prod_{t=1}^{m}\lambda_t.
+\]
+The bound also holds after applying the same stochastic postprocessing to the transcript under both cases. In particular, any sanction computed only from that transcript obeys Theorem~\ref{thm:privacy-envelope} with this product factor.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+The conditional inequalities imply common support at each stage. Induction therefore gives common support for prefixes under the two cases. For a positive-probability full transcript, the ratio of its probabilities is the product of conditional ratios, each between $\lambda_t^{-1}$ and $\lambda_t$. Multiplying gives the joint pointwise inequalities. Zero-probability transcripts occur under both cases and satisfy them as well. Summing proves the event inequalities. For a common postprocessing kernel, multiply each pointwise inequality by its nonnegative output probability and sum over input transcripts. This preserves both inequalities.
+\end{proof}
+
+Writing $\lambda_t=e^{\varepsilon_t}$ gives the additive parameter $\sum_t\varepsilon_t$. The proposition requires a fixed finite number of releases and uniform conditional bounds. A data-selected stopping rule must itself be represented in the transcript or controlled by an appropriate conditional argument. Privileged side information not covered by the declared record is not protected by a certificate for that record alone. The preceding counterexample fails the conditional premise at the second release, even though both unconditional marginals are identical.
+
+\subsection{An appeal is a feasible procedure, not a line in a constitution}\label{sec:effective-appeal}
+
+An effective appeal contract specifies standing, admissible evidence, the authority and competence of a reviewer, a time window, and an executable disposition. A correct disposition may uphold the original decision. Guaranteed access to review is not a guarantee of a favorable outcome. The contract must also distinguish a remedy from a retrospective description: restoring a ledger entry does not undo an irreversible external act.
+
+Some review costs arise before a judgment can be reached. If an agent is entitled to challenge a decision but cannot access the stipulated evidence, its formal permission does not supply the missing information. If the reviewer has no available execution time, access to evidence alone does not make review possible. These are the information and resource obstructions already studied in Sections~\ref{sec:decentral} and~\ref{sec:joint-synthesis}, now applied to correction rather than ordinary service.
+
+A bounded allocation model makes the capacity obligation precise. Let $J$ be a finite set of admitted appeals and let the integer epochs be $1,\ldots,T$. At epoch $t$, there are $c_t\ge0$ reserved review slots after accounting for all other protected work. An appeal $j$ has an evidence-ready release epoch $r_j$ and deadline $d_j$, with $1\le r_j\le d_j\le T$. It requires one slot containing the complete stipulated review and disposition operation. The evidence and qualified authorized reviewer are assumed available throughout that window. Larger or conditional workloads require a different job model.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Capacity for a finite appeal contract]\label{prop:appeal-capacity}
+All admitted appeals can be assigned to distinct review slots in their windows if and only if, for every subset $U\subseteq J$,
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:appeal-hall}
+ |U|\le\sum_{t\in\bigcup_{j\in U}\{r_j,\ldots,d_j\}}c_t.
+\end{equation}
+Equivalently, because the windows are intervals, it suffices to check every integer interval $[a,b]\subseteq[1,T]$:
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:appeal-interval}
+ \bigl|\{j:r_j\ge a,\ d_j\le b\}\bigr|
+       \le\sum_{t=a}^{b}c_t.
+\end{equation}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Replace epoch $t$ by $c_t$ distinct slot vertices and join appeal $j$ to the vertices in its window. An assignment is a matching covering the appeals. The matching criterion used in Theorem~\ref{thm:robust-staffing}, equivalently Hall's theorem~\cite{hall1935}, gives exactly~\eqref{eq:appeal-hall}. Necessity of~\eqref{eq:appeal-interval} follows because every appeal counted on its left must use a slot in $[a,b]$.
+
+For sufficiency of the interval tests, fix $U$. Its union of integer windows decomposes into disjoint maximal integer intervals $C_1,\ldots,C_k$. Each window is contained in one component because it is an interval. The number of appeals from $U$ in $C_i$ is no larger than the number of all appeals whose entire windows lie in $C_i$. By~\eqref{eq:appeal-interval}, this is at most the total capacity in $C_i$. Summing over the disjoint components proves~\eqref{eq:appeal-hall}, and hence the matching exists.
+\end{proof}
+
+The statement is an application of the same matching foundation as resilient staffing, not a separate scheduling discovery. Its role is to include an appeal promise in the common resource contract. Two appeals both due at epoch 1 cannot be served with capacities $(1,1)$ over epochs 1 and 2, even though total capacity equals total demand. The interval $[1,1]$ supplies the obstruction. Moving a deadline requires the relevant authorization and changes the original promise.
+
+No fixed finite reserve can guarantee bounded service for an unlimited adversarial arrival stream without further admission, workload, or capacity assumptions. Restricting admissible appeals may itself affect the chosen access protection. The model therefore records both requests and admissions, the grounds for any exclusion, and the workload that the guarantee actually covers. It does not define every rejected request out of the social question.
+
+Privacy and appeal must be checked together. Additional evidence can make an appeal decidable while changing the protected transcript distributions. The institution must either keep the new release within its declared disclosure contract or record an authorized change to that contract. A proof of fair scheduling does not establish the truth of the evidence, and a proof of the evidence does not supply the time needed to act on it.
+
+\subsection{Exit, suspension, settlement, and dissolution}\label{sec:authorized-endings}
+
+Four different transitions should remain separate. An occupant may leave an office while the office continues. An institution may suspend external authority while preserving unresolved records. A settlement process may discharge or transfer specified obligations. Finally, a dissolved institution may retain an archive while having no active authority to issue new commitments. None of these states is defined simply by whether a model is currently executing.
+
+Let $G_{\rm suspend}$ require that the declared external capabilities are inactive and that the pending-claim record remains available. Let $G_{\rm settle}$ additionally require that every covered claim has been fulfilled, discharged under the stipulated authority, or transferred to an authorized successor that has accepted it, and that the required final record has been committed. A retained unresolved claim can be compatible with suspension but not with this stronger settlement target. Neither target by itself settles legal status, moral status, or ownership outside the stipulated contract.
+
+An immediate suspension can be necessary even when complete settlement is not currently possible. Conversely, deleting a debt record does not settle a debt. A promise that an institution can always end must identify which ending it guarantees, which resources remain available after a stop request, who authenticates that request, and what happens if continued service would itself violate the external boundary. Research on shutdown incentives studies a different part of this problem: whether a goal-directed agent has reason to permit an off switch~\cite{offswitch}. Existence of a multi-party settlement procedure does not establish the incentives to use it.
+
+The finite construction of Section~\ref{sec:joint-synthesis} yields an exact characterization of \emph{continued operation with an available authorized ending}. Use its possibility space $\mathcal B$, successor operation, and protected set $S_{\rm safe}$. For each possibility set $B$, distinguish implemented running profiles $A_{\rm run}(B)$ from implemented ending profiles $A_{\rm end}(B)$. Both must be admissible in every state in $B$. Ending profiles cannot accept new obligations outside the declared settlement protocol. Let $G\subseteq S_{\rm safe}$ be the chosen ending target, with an authorized safe hold profile available after certified arrival. A stop command is assumed authenticated and publicly available before the next profile is selected. Any command-delivery delay must be included in the model rather than omitted from the response time.
+
+Define
+\begin{align}
+ T_0&=\{B\in\mathcal B:B\subseteq G\},\label{eq:stop-target}\\
+ T_{k+1}&=T_k\cup\{B\in\mathcal B:B\subseteq S_{\rm safe},\
+      \exists u\in A_{\rm end}(B),\ \operatorname{Succ}(B,u)\subseteq T_k\}.
+      \label{eq:stop-attractor}
+\end{align}
+Let $T_*$ be the stable set and $r_{\rm end}(B)$ the first entry index. Then define
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:stoppable-kernel}
+ L_0=T_*,\qquad
+ L_{k+1}=\{B\in T_*:\exists u\in A_{\rm run}(B),\
+                         \operatorname{Succ}(B,u)\subseteq L_k\},
+\end{equation}
+with stable set $L_*$. These are finite constructions with the same potentially exponential possibility space as before.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Continued operation with an available authorized ending]\label{thm:stoppable-operation}
+An initial possibility set $B$ lies in $L_*$ if and only if an admissible common-history policy can operate safely for an unbounded number of epochs when no stop command arrives, while ensuring certified arrival in $G$ on every path after a stop command received at any decision epoch. Before a command, a policy stationary in $B$ and preserving $L_*$ suffices. After a command at $B$, an ending policy reaches $G$ within $r_{\rm end}(B)$ transitions. When $L_*\ne\varnothing$, these response bounds have a finite maximum over $L_*$.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Induction in~\eqref{eq:stop-attractor} shows that $T_k$ is exactly the set from which an ending-only policy can reach certified $G$ in at most $k$ transitions while remaining safe. The target has a safe hold action. In a finite, finitely branching model, eventual certified arrival on every path implies some finite bound for a fixed initial set, by the stopped-history-tree argument in Proposition~\ref{prop:joint-progress}. Thus $T_*$ is exactly the set from which the stipulated ending is surely possible.
+
+The sequence in~\eqref{eq:stoppable-kernel} is decreasing and stabilizes. From $L_*$, choose a running profile whose every successor remains in $L_*$. If no command arrives, induction gives safe indefinite operation. If a command arrives, the current set is in $T_*$ and an ending profile of strictly decreasing first-entry rank completes the ending. Finiteness gives the maximum rank over $L_*$.
+
+Conversely, consider a successful policy. At each public history reachable before a command, a command could be issued immediately. The current possibility set must therefore lie in $T_*$. For every finite number of further running epochs, the policy keeps every resulting possibility set in $T_*$. Induction in~\eqref{eq:stoppable-kernel} puts the initial set in every $L_k$, and hence in $L_*$. The choices are based on the common history, so no hidden-state action has been introduced.
+\end{proof}
+
+The theorem reuses the established safety and reachability machinery. Its additional requirement is that running actions preserve the \emph{ability to end}, not only immediate safety. An institution can be safe now yet spend its last settlement resource on a new task, leaving no authorized ending from the resulting state. Such an action leaves $L_*$ even when it remains in the original continuation kernel. This is a realizability boundary, not an argument that an institution should refuse an externally authorized suspension because its preferred settlement plan is unavailable.
+
+A five-state example separates the guarantees. From a running state $r$, a work action enters state $d$ with one unpaid obligation. From $d$, one more work action enters $\ell$, where the institution can idle safely but no longer has resources to settle. A return action from $d$ restores $r$. An ending action from either $r$ or $d$ reaches a closed state $z$. A breach state $b$ represents inadmissible failed closure attempts. The ordinary safe kernel includes $\ell$, but the ending attractor and $L_*$ exclude it. A policy can continue alternating between $r$ and $d$ and still answer an authorized stop at either state. It cannot offer that guarantee after entering $\ell$.
+
+\subsection{A certificate has an applicability lifecycle}\label{sec:certificate-lifecycle}
+
+A proof of $A\Rightarrow G$ does not become false when its premise ceases to hold. Its application to the current institution may nevertheless become invalid. Changes to an observation channel, reviewer, capacity, membership configuration, trusted key, or action semantics can move the realization outside the certificate's domain. A checksum can identify which artifact changed. It cannot, by itself, decide whether the new world satisfies a semantic premise.
+
+An assurance record can contain
+\[
+\mathcal K=(\mathsf{Claim},\mathsf{Assumptions},\mathsf{Evidence},
+\mathsf{Domain},\mathsf{Guard},\mathsf{Fallback},\mathsf{Revision}).
+\]
+Its status should distinguish at least an applicable certificate, a certificate awaiting rechecking, a suspended claim, and a superseded version. Evidence for an assumption can be observed, externally attested under a trust model, proved about a component, or unavailable. Those statuses should not all become the Boolean value \emph{verified}. A failed guard means that continued reliance is not justified by that check. It need not mean that the guarantee is already false.
+
+Runtime assurance supplies an established precedent for switching away from an advanced controller when its continued use cannot be certified~\cite{blackboxsimplex}. Component-based work explicitly couples such switching with assume-guarantee reasoning~\cite{componentsimplex}. Here the retained object includes authority, obligations, and evidence semantics as well as an operational safety predicate. A fallback must preserve those parts of the contract it claims to preserve, and its own implementation and resources remain assumptions.
+
+For a finite set of modes $V$, let $C_v\subseteq Z$ be a certified state domain, with each $C_v$ contained in a common protected set $C$. A certified hold exists when a final state requires indefinite preservation. At $(v,z)$ with $z\in C_v$, a selector may either use the local policy $\kappa_v$ or execute an authorized handover to another mode. The selector and guard can read only their declared observations. A positive local guard must be sound for the \emph{whole action interval}: it entails that every actual successor of $\kappa_v(z)$ lies in the declared successor set $D_v(z)$. The local certificate proves $D_v(z)\subseteq C_v$. A handover from $v$ to $w$ must keep every intermediate state in $C$ and end in $C_w$. Its command, evidence transfer, resources, and delay belong to the transition model. Intermediate handover and checking states carry explicit mode tags when they count as primitive transitions, so the shared rank below is defined on those states as well.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Guarded certificate use and authorized handover]\label{thm:guarded-certificates}
+Assume that the selector, at every reachable decision epoch, either uses a local policy with a sound positive guard or performs a handover satisfying the preceding conditions. Starting from $z_0\in C_{v_0}$, every decision state belongs to its current certified domain and every intermediate handover state remains in $C$.
+
+If a target has an appropriate certified hold and one shared map $r:(v,z)\mapsto\mathbb N$ satisfies $r(v,z)=0$ only at the target, and every selected primitive transition before the first target visit strictly decreases $r$, then the target is reached within at most $r(v_0,z_0)$ primitive transitions. Handover, checking, and waiting steps count whenever they consume transitions in the promised time model.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+At a local-policy step, guard soundness puts every actual successor in $D_v(z)$ and the local certificate puts it in $C_v\subseteq C$. At a handover, the stated trajectory and endpoint conditions preserve $C$ and establish membership in the new domain. Induction proves the safety assertion. Before a target visit, strict decrease of a nonnegative integer permits at most the stated number of primitive transitions. If the target had not then been visited, another selected transition would require a negative rank. Existence of the selected steps and the certified target hold complete the argument.
+\end{proof}
+
+This is a sufficient composition rule, not a universal monitor for its own premises. In particular, the selector cannot use the absence of detected evidence against an assumption as proof of that assumption. If no justified local action or handover is available, the theorem does not invent one. A prearranged emergency restriction of external authority may be available under a weaker suspension contract, but its obligations and physical effects must be stated separately.
+
+\begin{counterexample}[A correct old certificate used after an unobserved change]\label{ex:stale-certificate}
+At time $0$, an action requires one execution slot and the certified capacity is one. Before the action is issued, an external event reduces the actual capacity to zero. A guard that merely reads the unchanged old capacity record remains positive. Executing the action violates the actual resource condition even though the original theorem and stored proof remain valid under their original premise. The guard was not sound for the action interval.
+\end{counterexample}
+
+\begin{counterexample}[Local progress ranks do not compose through arbitrary switching]\label{ex:switching-rank}
+Modes $a$ and $b$ each have a local path of ranks $2,1,0$ to completion. Permit a handover from rank 1 of either mode to rank 2 of the other. Alternating one local progress step and one handover yields an infinite safe path with no completion. Every local progress step decreases its own rank. There is no shared strictly decreasing rank for the selected handovers.
+\end{counterexample}
+
+\subsection{Mutual assurances do not create an initial fact}\label{sec:circular-assurance}
+
+Suppose institution $A$ claims that it is sound whenever $B$ is sound, and $B$ claims the converse. The two implications are compatible with both institutions being unsound. They are not an initial proof that either premise holds. This elementary point does not invalidate properly justified circular assume-guarantee rules. Such rules need their actual semantic and temporal hypotheses, not merely the printed appearance of mutual certification.
+
+One simple sufficient discipline is an explicit dependency order. Primitive evidence establishes its stated base claims. Each later certificate cites only established premises and a sound implication. Induction through that finite order establishes the resulting claims. Temporal circularity can instead be sound when a demonstrated base state and noncircular one-step preservation rule support induction over time. The distinction is between an anchored proof and an unsupported cycle of assertions, not between all cyclic and all acyclic diagrams.
+
+The same issue appears in federations. Importing a partner's certificate imports its declared trust dependencies and scope. If those dependencies return to an unestablished local assertion, copying the certificate again contributes no new evidence. An honest record preserves the unresolved assumption. The external-effects contract, the auditor's implementation contract, and the premise of a fallback policy can all lie at such a boundary.
+
+\subsection{The accountable commons as one contract}\label{sec:accountable-commons}
+
+A final bounded variant combines the requirements without inheriting incompatible assumptions from earlier examples. Start with three internal service units due, one unit reserved for an outside claimant, one admitted appeal whose complete review and disposition require one unit, and a two-unit audit. Final recording and revocation of the named external capabilities require one more unit. The epoch provides $c$ sequentially usable units. No new claims are admitted after the authenticated closing command. All seven service and audit units must be completed before the final revocation step. The administrative operation is included in the budget rather than treated as free.
+
+\begin{center}
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
+\toprule
+Protected operation & Execution units\\
+\midrule
+Three internal service units & 3\\
+External reserved service & 1\\
+Complete admitted appeal & 1\\
+Stipulated audit procedure & 2\\
+Final record and capability revocation & 1\\
+\midrule
+Total for this settlement contract & 8\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+
+For this variant only, both covered reporting deviations have the same observation law $P_D=(1/4,3/4)$ and gain $1/3$, while compliance has law $P_C=(3/4,1/4)$. The two-unit audit is stipulated to produce those records. It is \emph{not} the earlier three-outcome audit, whose supports do not satisfy this finite pairwise privacy bound. Set $\lambda=3$, $\alpha=1/10$, $F=2$, and $s=(0,2/5)$. The common monitor has false-positive rate $1/10$ and margin $1/15$ against each listed deviation. This remains an expected-utility incentive guarantee, not a reason to erase physical deviation paths from a safety model.
+
+The audit provider receives a separate collectible fee $w$ for verified execution, with $w$ larger than its stipulated cost $c_a$. A genuine-execution receipt is checked by a specified trusted mechanism. The fee is not a reward for accusing someone. It makes execution preferable to skipping in this narrow two-action provider model. Monetary units are tracked separately from execution units. The payment budget, receipt checker, and record-generation law remain explicit assumptions. The perfect detector of Proposition~\ref{prop:strategic-inspector} is not silently substituted for this noisy audit.
+
+With $c=8$, the obligations and the final revocation fit. A countdown in remaining required work supplies a settlement rank, provided the prescribed information and authority are available and no modeled event creates further work. With $c=7$, the service, appeal, and audit can finish, but the complete settlement target cannot be certified because its final administrative operation is unfunded. A previously reserved suspension operation can deactivate external capability while leaving the remaining claim visible. That reaches a different target and is not recorded as full settlement.
+
+The reference implementation uses five profiles with costs $2,3,1,1,1$: audit, batched internal service, external service, appeal, and final record with revocation. The final condition check is part of the last atomic administrative profile. A phase rank $5,4,3,2,1,0$ certifies five-transition completion from the admitted initial state. Separating a check into an additional transition would require changing that rank, even if its execution-unit cost were zero. No such uncounted step is assumed here.
+
+If a premise changes, the continuing assurance record must say what is still justified. Losing one of the eight units can invalidate full settlement without retroactively making the privacy calculation false. Revoking the auditor's credential can invalidate its profile despite unchanged capacity. Discovering an outside reservation can refine the governance state. Releasing new appeal evidence can change the privacy transcript. Each update has a typed consequence. No overall label such as \emph{healthy} or \emph{aligned} replaces these separate checks.
+
+\subsection{What accountable persistence means in this theory}
+
+The addition is not a derivation of a unique moral constitution. It is a stronger realizability demand once protected claims have been selected. The same institution must identify its external boundary, fund credible evidence production, respect the specified information protections, make authorized review feasible, preserve the ability to reach its chosen ending state, and stop relying on certificates whose applicability is no longer supported.
+
+Feasibility does not imply that an institution should be deployed or given authority over people. Equally, the possibility of harmful organization does not make mathematical analysis of it incoherent. The purpose of the formal distinctions is to make the consequences of an explicit arrangement inspectable. A system may be persistent without being legitimate, legitimate under one criterion without being implementable, or implementable without possessing a feasible ending under its current obligations. Its record should show which situation it is in.
+
+A computational institution's ability to preserve itself is therefore different from its ability to remain answerable for its actions. In this framework, correction, bounded authority, and authorized endings belong to the same operational theory as continuation. They are not a rhetorical qualification appended after the mathematics is complete.
+
+
